   "source": [
    "There's a lot to unpack in the code below, so let's talk about the code step-by-step. We are working with multiple data sources rather than just 1, so we have to do a little bit extra up front to account for that. \n",
    "\n",
    "URL part: This is where we create a dictionary where the key is the name of the movie, and the value is the URL where the script is coming from.\n",
    "\n",
    "Where it says \"store scripts\", that's an empty list. Since we have multiple items, we need to initialize that to be able to store them.\n",
    "\n",
    "Also, under \"header\", there is something that says \"User-Agent\". It basically shows you're not a bot. If you ever have issues, you can Google \"what is my user agent\" and paste your real one here, but the short version I use usually works fine.\n",
    "\n",
    "The \"for loop\" is basically a repeated pathway that goes through each movie and URL in the dictionary we set up and gets the script automatically, so that we do not have to run it 11 times separately. It says \"if\" and then \"else\", which means \"IF things work nicely, do this, ELSE let me know what part went wrong\".\n",
    "\n",
    "You might be wondering, how does beautiful soup know where to look for text? That's the part where 'div', class_='scrolling-script-container' comes in. If you visit one of the URLs, highlight some of the script, right-click, and select \"Inspect\", you'll see that the movie dialogue is inside a <\"div\"> with the class name \"scrolling-script-container\". That’s what we tell BeautifulSoup to look for!\n",
    "\n",
    "The part at the bottom that says \"time.sleep\" is where you randomize how many seconds of a break you take between each URL retrieval. It's good to take a break between each to make your behavior look less bot-like."
   ]
  },
